| Path | Top author | Share | Second |
|---|---|---|---|
| `lib/` | Troy Goode | 109/181 = 60.2% | Douglas Wilson 19.9% |
| `test/` | Troy Goode | 411/786 = 52.3% | Douglas Wilson 38.8% |
| `.github/` | Douglas Wilson | 108/366 = 29.5% | Íñigo Marquínez Prado 25.7% |
| root files | Troy Goode | 122/415 = 29.4% | Douglas Wilson 27.7% |

Suggested CODEOWNERS:

```
# Troy Goode holds >=50% of surviving lines
/lib/   @troygoode
/test/  @troygoode

# No owner: nobody holds 50% of surviving lines
# /.github/   (top: Douglas Wilson 29.5%)
# /*          (root files; top: Troy Goode 29.4%)
```

`@troygoode` is a guess at the GitHub handle from the name, so check it. I only have commit emails, not handles.

- **`.github/` and root files:** No one holds half, so I name no owner. Authorship is spread across many people. Add a team handle if you want a default reviewer.
- **`test/` is borderline:** Troy's 52.3% includes 36 lines from `test/vary.js`. His commit `02afce3` ("vary header coverage") added that file. If you don't count that file, Troy drops to about 50%. He still just reaches half.
- **`lib/` is safer:** Troy's 60.2% includes 12 `// checked` comment lines from `b2005d9`. Those are real edits, not formatting, so I counted them. Without them he is still above 50%.
- **Reformat commit:** Blame can't map some reflowed lines back to their original authors. Dropping the formatter's lines removes those lines from the count entirely. It doesn't credit them to anyone.

